{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## What the ticked acceptance criteria mean\n\nAll ten boxes are now ticked. To be precise about who earned which, since a partial fix for\nthis class landed first as `8c88ddc006` (bug dca1):\n\n**Ticked by this change (nine of ten, outright):** AC1 (`grep -rl 'receive.autogc' tests/`\nnow returns exactly `tests/_git_upkeep.py`, down from two files), AC2, AC3, AC4, AC5, AC7,\nAC8, AC9, AC10.\n\n**AC6 is the one with shared credit.** It asks for a no-detached-upkeep guard *per site*.\n`8c88ddc006` had already written that guard for the `test_rebar_repo_isolation.py` site —\nwhich is not one of this ticket's two HIGH sites. This change wrote the guard for both HIGH\nsites (`test_prepare_reclaim_backup.py`, `test_inline_write_push.py`) and repointed dca1's\nexisting one at the shared helper, whose positive control it also strengthened. So the box is\nticked because the criterion now holds for every pinned site, not because I authored all\nthree guards.\n\nNothing in the landed partial fix satisfied any AC outright; it is, however, the reason AC1's\ncount started at 2 rather than 1, since it added the second copy of the pins.\n\n## Two corrections to the description, recorded rather than edited\n\n1. **`init_repo` does not push.** The description says `test_inline_write_push.py`'s template\n   is exposed because \"`rebar.init_repo(...)` pushes under the default `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always`\".\n   Measured: after `init_repo`, `origin.git` has no refs and the trace records no\n   `receive-pack`. The first push comes from the first WRITE, which happens in the per-test\n   COPY. The site is still genuinely exposed — every test's write pushes into an unpinned\n   bare origin while `_ahead` fetches from the same object database on the next statement —\n   but via a different event than described. That site's guard therefore drives a write, not\n   just the build.\n\n2. **A whole-build trace cannot be used as the oracle.** The description's AC6 wording\n   (\"running the push under `GIT_TRACE2_EVENT`, asserting no `git maintenance run` child\n   carries `--detach`\") is exactly right for a scoped push and wrong for a whole fixture\n   build. Driving `_build_backup_fixture` under one trace fails with 7 detached children even\n   once `source.git` is pinned: they belong to `seed`, a NON-bare repository outside the\n   copied topology that nothing copies. trace2 reports no worktree for a bare repository, so\n   a maintenance child cannot be attributed to its spawning repo after the fact. Each guard\n   therefore traces only the push it means to judge, and the shared helper's docstring says\n   so. The positive control is correspondingly strengthened: it now requires a\n   `git-receive-pack` recorded against THAT remote, not merely the substring `receive-pack`\n   anywhere in the trace."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787298249728634001,"uuid":"2b50aca7-c3ba-4fa9-8029-453b62ba1a3c"}